e to grow the company so  quickly and so into such a tremendous  size because in the background china's  economy was booming china was trying to  make room for hundreds of millions of  people moving from the countryside into  the cities  that was feeling a boom in demand for  property for homes for apartments  at the same time  as a result of the financial crisis in  2008 china introduced this massive  stimulus package that flooded the  economy with money and made  lending very easy very cheap the company  was able to translate some of its land  holdings use that as collateral for  loans to then help it get control of  more land and then to get more loans and  sort of it was a cycle that went like  that  through massive borrowing today  evergrande has become a sprawling  conglomerate that controls land five  times the size of manhattan and owns  more than 1300 real estate projects in  280 cities  it has businesses in a new energy  vehicles and historically also has  invested in football companies and even  bottled water  but as chinese president xi jinping  began to control financial risks in the  country's economy huay's debt-field  expansion strategy was called into  question  starting in late 2015-16 there was this  greater emphasis on curbing the amount  of debt in the chinese system by lots of  different measurements there is a  tremendous amount of debt in china it's  not just the amount of debt that china  has it's the pace at which it's  accumulated that debt  there is a real concern if chinese  companies borrow a lot of money and  build bridges to nowhere roads that  people don't use  homes that no one wants to live in  then that money is wasted and when they  have to pay it back there's not going to  be any money there to pay it back with  and  that is going to admire the economy  a big part of the debt problem lies in  china's real estate sector which  accounts for about 30 percent of the  country's dollar-denominated bonds  to curb reckless borrowing in 